# Creating folders and files. Everything goes through NewItemPlanner/NewItemExecutor — the
# same code the New menu, the folder tree and Ctrl+Shift+N go through — so a rule mutated in
# either shows up here. The refusals themselves are asserted in NewItemPlannerTests; a script
# stops on the first failing command, so this covers the wiring instead.

tree .
refresh
assert-count 8

# A new folder lands in the list, on disk, and selected. That last one is the PendingSelection
# hand-off: the shell sets it before awaiting the refresh, so the listing that arrives is the
# one the selection belongs to. Set it afterwards and this goes red.
newfolder Reports
settle
assert-row Reports
assert-exists Reports
assert-selected 1
assert-count 9
shot 01-new-folder

# The suggested name steps aside rather than opening on a name it would refuse — "New folder"
# exists after the first one, so the second is "New folder (2)".
newfolder "New folder"
settle
newfolder "New folder (2)"
settle
assert-row "New folder"
assert-row "New folder (2)"

# A new file. The name is taken literally, extension and all.
newfile "Meeting notes.txt"
settle
assert-row "Meeting notes.txt"
assert-exists "Meeting notes.txt"

# Creating in an empty folder, which is where a New menu is most often reached for.
enter Empty
settle
assert-count 0
newfolder Drafts
settle
assert-count 1
assert-row Drafts
up
settle

# Creating never claims the undo slot — it is additive, like Copy. The interesting half is the
# second assertion: a rename before it stays undoable, which is what "does not touch the slot"
# means. Call RetireUndoable in CreateNewItemAsync and this goes red.
assert-cannot-undo
select notes.txt
rename renamed.txt
assert-can-undo
newfolder Ideas
settle
assert-row Ideas
assert-can-undo
undo
settle
assert-row notes.txt

# Both dialogs, with their suggested names filled in. Neither needs a selection.
deselect
dialog new-folder 02-new-folder-dialog
dialog new-file 03-new-file-dialog

assert-not-launched
state
